          body: 'Cohesion is the sticking of water molecules to each other. It creates surface tension, which lets a water strider skate on a pond, and it helps pull unbroken columns of water up the xylem of tall trees. Adhesion is the sticking of water to other polar surfaces, such as the walls of those xylem tubes, which stops the column from sagging.\n\nWater also has a high specific heat: it takes about 4.18 joules to warm 1 gram of water by 1 °C, far more than most liquids, because added heat first goes into breaking hydrogen bonds instead of speeding up molecules. So large bodies of water, and our own bodies, change temperature slowly. Water also has a high heat of vaporization: about 2.4 kJ is absorbed to evaporate each gram at body temperature. This is why sweating cools you.\n\nFinally, ice floats. Most liquids become denser when they freeze, but in ice each molecule is locked into a rigid, open lattice with four hydrogen bonds, which spaces molecules slightly further apart than in liquid water. Liquid water is densest at about 4 °C, so ponds freeze from the top down and the ice insulates the life below.',
